in scattered ash, oil stains, and dried flowers looks cluttered and unclean. But for the person living there, the presence of those items might signify that the space is active, lived-in, and spiritually vibrant. So, your observation that the physical byproducts of rituals create a specific kind of clutter is entirely logical and grounded in reality. Where we still diverge is just on the final conclusion. The presence of ritual clutter explains why a pooja room or a traditional house might look disorganized or have a build-up of specific materials. It is a byproduct of a religious practice. But it doesn't mean the people themselves are inherently "dirtier" as a community—it just means they are engaging in a daily practice that generates a very specific, culturally complicated type of physical residue.
